{"type":"message","id":"7b21b127","parentId":"cce6e1e4","timestamp":"2026-03-30T10:00:32.580Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[[reply_to_current]] Got it — no recent medication change makes a new side effect from a dose change less likely, though your current medicines and blood pressure still remain relevant.\n\n## Recommended questions\n**(4/4)** Have you had any **recent labs or checkups** in the last few months, and have you noticed **swelling in your legs/ankles, reduced exercise tolerance, nausea, or changes in urination**?","textSignature":"{\"v\":1,\"id\":\"msg_0ec33241ee8fa4270169ca49be365c81919d280b4c864bf0a0\",\"phase\":\"final_answer\"}"}],"api":"openai-codex-responses","provider":"openai-codex","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":319,"output":93,"cacheRead":19584,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":19996,"cost":{"input":0.0007975,"output":0.001395,"cacheRead":0.004896,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0070885}},"stopReason":"stop","timestamp":1774864827336}}
